<p>Hey all —<p>Sharing a thought experiment I put together. The premise: what might declarative programming feel like for the physical world?<p>We’re already familiar with this paradigm in software land (eg SQL etc where we declare what we want and the execution engine figures out how).<p>I didn’t have access to an actuator so I mocked up the concept in software. The way it works is you demonstrate the effect and the system figures out how to achieve it. So say you want to ring a bell and you have a crank. The system will find a way to connect the two. If you add obstacles it figures out how to work around them (runs a constraint solver runs behind the scenes).<p>I think what’s interesting about this is being able to think about the problem at a higher level of abstraction, with the system figuring out a way to achieve the intended effect.<p>Anyways, curious to read reactions, feedback, and interesting ideas on how to extend this!</p>
<hr>
